This is Java JALoP Network Library (JJNL) v1.x.

JJNL v1.x implements a library that supports sending and receiving of
JALoP Records over a network using the JALoP over BEEP protocol. Simply put,
JJNL v1.x is a JALoP v1.x (JALoP over BEEP) Publisher and Subscriber.

Building and Installing:

This project uses the maven (maven.apache.org) build tool version 3.8.6, and
is tested using OpenJDK 8, 11, 17, and 21. Any other versions of maven should
work, but have not been tested.

The JJNL uses a multi-module layout, and the entire project can be built by
running maven commands in the jnl_parent directory. The various modules are:

 - jnl_parent - This is the top-level project. Running maven commands within
      this directory affects all modules in the project. The jnl_parent module
      contains common configuration and dependency information.

 - jnl_lib - This module contains the code for the Java JALoP Network Library.

 - jnl_test - This module contains a utility program that acts as a JALoP Network
      Store. It uses the JNL to either listen for incoming connections, or
      initiate a connection to a remote. It's behaviour is governed by a
      configuration file.

Installation Instructions:

1. Install maven:
    % yum install maven

2. Install the target beepcore library
    # Go to the root jjnl directory and run the following commands to determine the required beepcore version:
        ver=$(gawk 'BEGIN{RS="<[^>]+>"}{if (RT=="</beepcore.version>"){print $1}}' jnl_parent/pom.xml)
        echo $ver

    # Install beepcore from the jjnl 3rd-party distribution directory
        cd 3rd-party/beepcore-java/${ver}
	gunzip beepcore-${ver}.tgz
        tar xvf beepcore-${ver}.tar
        cd beepcore-${ver}/lib
        chmod 744 ./install_jars.sh
        ./install_jars.sh

3. Package JALoP JNL project
	# Go to the root directory for the jjnl repository.
	# Go into the jnl_parent subdirectory:
        cd ../../../../../jnl_parent

	# Build the jjnl library using maven:
		mvn clean
		mvn package

	# Note:
	# To build using specific Java version and bypass SSL certificate validation, run the following mvn command, for example -
	# mvn -Dmaven.wagon.http.ssl.insecure=true -Djava.version="17" -U clean package

	# Note:
	# To skip the unit tests, run the following mvn command -
		mvn package -DskipTests=true

Logging:
--------
To change the logging level, you can either set the `log4j2.level` system property at the command line (e.g.):

     java -Dlog4j2.level=DEBUG -jar target/jnl_test-1.2.0.0.jar ./sampleSubscriber.json

or set the `log4j2.configurationFile` system property and pass in a custom `log4j2.xml` file (e.g.):

    java -Dlog4j2.configurationFile=log4j2.debug.xml -jar target/jnl_test-1.2.0.0.jar ./sampleSubscriber.json

The default logging level is INFO.

To append to a file, you can redirect output using Bash as follows:

    java -Dlog4j2.configurationFile=log4j2.debug.xml -jar target/jnl_test-1.2.0.0.jar ./sampleSubscriber.json 1>output.log 2>error.log

Or you can update the custom `log4j2.xml` by adding a File Appender as follows:

    <Appenders>
        ...
        <File name="LogFile" fileName="out.log" immediateFlush="true" append="false">
            <PatternLayout pattern="%d{yyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSS} [%t] %-5level %logger{36} - %msg%n"/>
        </File>
    </Appenders>


And then add an AppenderRef for `LogFile` under Loggers/Root:

    <Root level="info">
        ...
        <AppenderRef ref="LogFile"/>
    </Root>


Other Options:
--------------
These steps do not need to be executed in order to use the JALoP
libraries.

To run the unit tests and generate code coverage reports, run:

    mvn test

The generated code coverage reports will appear in the directory coverage-report

To clean the target directory, run:

    mvn clean

To generate Javadoc documentation, run:

    mvn site

The generated documents will appear in the directory target/site.

This project is built and tested in the following environments:
  - RHEL 7 on x86_64
  - RHEL 8 on x86_64
  - RHEL 9 on x86_64
